&lt;p&gt;Most "free online image tools" upload your files to a server, keep them for some period, and often want an account. I wanted the opposite: every operation runs on your device, nothing is transmitted to a backend, and no account is required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why I built &lt;strong&gt;PixelShrink&lt;/strong&gt; — a browser-only image toolkit that runs entirely on the Canvas API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compress JPG, PNG, and WebP images&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resize, crop, and convert between formats&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compress to a target file size (100 KB, 200 KB)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Batch compress and download results as a ZIP&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Combine images into a PDF&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inspect and strip EXIF metadata&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Generate favicons, social-media presets, and passport sizes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;17 tools in total, all client-side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why browser-only matters
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Image compression isn't expensive. It's a CPU operation that any laptop can do in milliseconds. There's no technical reason to send a 3 MB photo to a server and back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reasons tools do it anyway are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;They want to show you an ad before the download.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;They want your email for their marketing list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;They want to upsell you to a paid plan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browser-only tools remove all three. The image never leaves your device. No upload, no account, no tracking.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The technical stack
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React + TypeScript&lt;/strong&gt; for the UI&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt; for the build&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/strong&gt; for styling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Canvas API&lt;/strong&gt; for all image processing&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;pdf-lib&lt;/strong&gt; for the image-to-PDF tool&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;jszip&lt;/strong&gt; for batch downloads&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;react-image-crop&lt;/strong&gt; for the cropper&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;exifr&lt;/strong&gt; for metadata reading&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything is lazy-loaded per route, so the home page loads in under 100 KB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The hardest part
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things were genuinely tricky:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Format detection&lt;/strong&gt; Browsers don't all support the same encoder set. Safari can encode WebP and AVIF, Firefox can encode WebP but AVIF support varies, and old browsers can't encode either. Rather than claim "AVIF supported!" and then silently fall back to PNG, the site runs a 2×2 test canvas through &lt;code&gt;toBlob&lt;/code&gt; at runtime and only offers formats the browser can actually produce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Touch-friendly cropping.&lt;/strong&gt; Getting a crop box to feel right on a phone — drag to resize, drag inside to move, snap to aspect ratio — is harder than it looks with raw pointer events. I used &lt;code&gt;react-image-crop&lt;/code&gt; rather than rolling my own, and it handles the touch edge cases correctly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I deliberately didn't do
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No server-side processing.&lt;/strong&gt; That would break the "no uploads" promise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No AI upscaling.&lt;/strong&gt; The upscaler uses plain canvas interpolation and says so on the page. Faking "AI enhancement" would be dishonest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No HEIC support.&lt;/strong&gt; Browsers can't decode HEIC without a large WASM bundle. I'd rather say "not supported" than ship a broken tool.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No analytics or ads (yet).&lt;/strong&gt; If I ever add them, the privacy policy will be updated first.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://pixelshrink-tools.netlify.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pixelshrink-tools.netlify.app/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No account, no uploads, no tracking. Free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love feedback — especially on the touch experience and on any format the site doesn't handle that you wish it did.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When we shipped hybrid mobile support in JOPS 1.0.13, we said the next step was to examine how JOPS and Next.js could work together. With &lt;strong&gt;jops-core 1.1.0&lt;/strong&gt;, that work is done: a complete JOPS single-page app can now run inside a page of a Next.js app, next to React, with Next.js Route Handlers as its backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This isn't JOPS competing with React. Next.js keeps everything it does best: server rendering, data fetching, routing and the React ecosystem. JOPS adds class-based views with an Android-style lifecycle on the pages that need them. One codebase, two models, each where it fits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why bring classes into a Next.js app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Object-oriented design is how most backend and mobile developers think: Java, C#, Kotlin, Swift, Android and UIKit are all built around classes. Modern React has moved the other way. Server Components are async functions, and React's newest features are built for function components. Classes still work in client components, but a class-based architecture no longer fits naturally in a modern React and Next.js codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Functions and hooks are a proven model, and many teams are productive with them. JOPS gives teams that think in classes a first-class place inside the same app, without asking anyone to switch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How it works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The split is simple:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Next.js owns the path, JOPS owns the hash.&lt;/strong&gt; The page &lt;code&gt;/quotes&lt;/code&gt; is a Next.js route; &lt;code&gt;/quotes#details?id=q2&lt;/code&gt; is a screen inside the JOPS app.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The JOPS app lives in &lt;code&gt;public/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and is served as-is. Turbopack and webpack never bundle it, so JOPS keeps its no-build design.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The backend is Next.js.&lt;/strong&gt; The JOPS app calls Route Handlers with &lt;code&gt;fetch()&lt;/code&gt;, on the same origin, with the session cookie sent automatically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bridge is a single React component, &lt;code&gt;JopsApp&lt;/code&gt;, imported from &lt;code&gt;jops-core/nextjs&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/app/quotes/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JopsApp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;jops-core/nextjs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;QuotesPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;JopsApp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"/quotes/App.js"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"quotes-root"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;JopsApp&lt;/code&gt; is a Client Component, so a Server Component page can render it. On the server it renders an empty &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. In the browser it loads the JOPS library once per page, adds the import map and starts the app inside that &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The app's entry module is the same &lt;code&gt;App.js&lt;/code&gt; a standalone JOPS app uses:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// public/quotes/App.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;View&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;jops-core&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;constructor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;View&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadLayout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/quotes/layout/main.html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Proven on a real application
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We didn't design the bridge on a whiteboard. We built a typical Next.js CRM first, on Next.js 16, React 19, the App Router and Tailwind CSS 4, and then rebuilt its entire Quote Builder as a JOPS app inside it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A quote list with status filters and loading skeletons&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Quote details with status actions and a printable quote document&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A two-step quote editor with a product picker, live totals and server-side validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A new-quote flow started from the CRM's own sales pipeline&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All of it runs on the CRM's REST Route Handlers, styled with the same Tailwind classes as the rest of the app. Building a real feature end to end exposed issues no demo would have. We fixed them in the framework before the release, and the few that are better handled by convention are listed below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  An app lifecycle that respects the host
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A JOPS app inside Next.js behaves like an app on a phone:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Leaving the page ends the app completely.&lt;/strong&gt; Follow a Next.js link elsewhere, or press Back past the app's first screen, and the router, every view, the app's &lt;code&gt;Store&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;EventBus&lt;/code&gt;, and JOPS's history hooks are all removed. Nothing keeps running in the background.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coming back launches it again&lt;/strong&gt; and restores the screen from the hash.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Next.js navigation keeps working.&lt;/strong&gt; Next.js history state is preserved in every entry JOPS writes, so Back and Forward behave across both frameworks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Get started
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quickest start is the new project template. In an empty folder:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx jops-core init
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# choose: 3. Next.js JOPS Embedded SPA&lt;/span&gt;
npm run dev
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It creates a Next.js project with a React-only page, a page hosting a JOPS SPA (a mock login and a quotes list), and a Route Handler as its backend. To add JOPS to an existing Next.js app instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm i jops-core
npx jops-core update   &lt;span class="c"&gt;# copies the library to public/lib/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then put your JOPS app in &lt;code&gt;public/&lt;/code&gt; and render &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;JopsApp app="/my-app/App.js" /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; on the page that hosts it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not using React? The same mechanism works on any page: load the library with &lt;code&gt;data-host&lt;/code&gt;, and start or stop the app yourself with &lt;code&gt;window.JOPS.mount()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;unmount()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Also new in 1.1.0
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building a full application inside Next.js shaped several additions that every JOPS app can use, standalone or hosted:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;onPause()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, the counterpart of &lt;code&gt;onResume()&lt;/code&gt;, called when another view comes to the front while this one stays alive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tag props:&lt;/strong&gt; attributes on a declared view, such as &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Card jops title="Hi" item-id="7"&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, arrive as read-only &lt;code&gt;this.props&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Layouts with several top-level elements&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;find(id)&lt;/code&gt; for every element with a given id.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Owned event subscriptions:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;EventBus.subscribe(owner, event, callback)&lt;/code&gt; removes itself when its view is destroyed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A round of router and view fixes&lt;/strong&gt; found while building the Quote Builder, from reliable default routes to views that resume exactly once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Know before you start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few conventions keep the two frameworks out of each other's way:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One JOPS app per page.&lt;/strong&gt; The router, &lt;code&gt;Store&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;EventBus&lt;/code&gt; are page-wide.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Root-absolute paths&lt;/strong&gt; for everything JOPS loads, and the library served from &lt;code&gt;public/lib/&lt;/code&gt; rather than a CDN.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Next.js owns the CSS.&lt;/strong&gt; Inside Next.js, &lt;code&gt;setStyle()&lt;/code&gt; is ignored; Tailwind picks up classes used in your JOPS files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;REST, not Server Actions.&lt;/strong&gt; A JOPS app talks to Next.js through Route Handlers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deploy &lt;code&gt;public/&lt;/code&gt; with the app,&lt;/strong&gt; and use short cache headers for the JOPS files there, since they aren't content-hashed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/jops-core" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;jops-core on npm&lt;/a&gt;: the full guide is in the README section "Next.js Integration"&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/uidb-dev/jops-core" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; for issues and discussions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://ui-db.com/articles/jops-class-based-spa-framework/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Introducing JOPS&lt;/a&gt;, if you're new to the framework&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://ui-db.com/articles/jops-hybrid-mobile-cordova-capacitor/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JOPS Goes Mobile&lt;/a&gt;, the hybrid iOS and Android release&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What's next: JOPS and Electron for standalone applications
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JOPS now runs in the browser, in hybrid mobile apps and inside Next.js. The next environment on our list is the desktop. The JOPS team will examine integration with Electron, so the same class-based views can power standalone applications on Windows, macOS and Linux, with access to the file system and native menus through Electron's main process. Many of the internal tools JOPS was built for live on the desktop, and we'll share what we learn as the work progresses.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;JOPS is built and maintained at UIDB, a boutique team of senior engineers who build custom systems with our clients, side by side. If you're extending a Next.js system or planning one that needs to hold up for years, &lt;a href="https://ui-db.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;we'd be glad to talk&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Talking first about the front-end framework Tauri, Tauri was first introduced in 2019 as an alternative to electron to leverage native web view of operating system instead of using chromium web view on all platform. This approach eats less memory. The fundamental approach of Tauri is to write your backend code in Rust which works very good cross-platform desktop application. However, when needing to run apps on the web which its possible to extract the front end and use REST api architecture to communicate between frontend and backend by using vite and the command pnpm run. This could save so much time by creating desktop, mobile, web application all in one front-end source code. In this article, I will explain how I built &lt;a href="https://ihives.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iHives&lt;/a&gt; a e-commerce platform. Using web tools such as Vite, Tauri, and FastApi.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The process is simple by following the documentation on Tauri v2 webpage. In &lt;a href="https://v2.tauri.app/start/create-project/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TauriV2&lt;/a&gt; you can select any package manager you are familiar with to install Tauri in my case I picked pnpm, I used to use npm, but after the breach this year I switched to pnpm. Anyway, afterwords you will be prompted to name the project, select front end language. Like so in the figure below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgb9r2wcmj70rufiber56.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgb9r2wcmj70rufiber56.png" alt=" " width="800" height="423"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am using reactJS for this project and installed Shadcn UI library to enhance ui/ux of the website. By using Shadcn plus Tailwind you can easier manipulate the screen responsive dimensions, so you can adjust it to each screen whether it's a mobile, web, or desktop screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the api endpoints, using python Fastapi with Redis to enhance response time to nearly microseconds. This architecture saved me a lot of time in configuration as one person working on the project. You can still use Rust code for the desktop, and mobile built, but when it comes for the web page you ought to use an api served on the server to execute business logic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How we're building a fraud-reporting experience with React, TypeScript, Supabase, and Gemini — and why human review matters more than a perfect-looking AI demo.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine receiving this message:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Your package couldn't be delivered. Pay €2.95 to arrange another delivery."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's a link underneath it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't recognize the sender. The website looks legitimate, but something feels wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now imagine opening a fraud-reporting website and seeing a form asking you to identify the incident category, communication channel, suspicious domain, financial impact, relevant evidence, and contact details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a developer, these fields probably make sense.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For someone who is worried about losing money, they might not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That difference became an important part of what we're building at &lt;strong&gt;Axon OSINT&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wanted people to report suspicious activity without first having to understand the vocabulary of fraud investigations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And we started with something almost everyone knows how to create:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A screenshot.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But we discovered that converting a screenshot into structured information is only one part of the problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more important question is what happens when the AI makes a mistake.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  1. The Problem Isn't Just Extracting Text
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's start with a simple example.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A screenshot of a suspicious message may contain several different kinds of information:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The original message.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A phone number or username.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A website address.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A payment amount.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The name of a company someone is impersonating.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A date or timestamp.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those details are not interchangeable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider the difference between these two statements:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Observed fact:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"The screenshot contains a link to &lt;code&gt;parcel-check.example&lt;/code&gt;."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interpretation:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"The link belongs to a fraudulent delivery service."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first statement describes something visible in the image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second makes a judgment about what it means.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction is critical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An AI system might correctly extract a URL while being wrong about the intentions of the sender. It might also misunderstand part of a message, confuse two numbers, or suggest the wrong incident category.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an ordinary productivity application, a slightly incorrect summary can be inconvenient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a fraud report, an incorrect phone number or website address can make the information significantly less useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So we designed the screenshot feature around a simple principle:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI should help organize what the user provides, not decide what happened on the user's behalf.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  2. Our Architecture: A Draft Before a Report
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our frontend uses React and TypeScript. Supabase handles parts of the backend, and Gemini is used for optional screenshot analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The simplified flow looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;User chooses screenshot upload
              |
              v
     File and age checks
              |
              v
   Screenshot analysis request
              |
              v
          Gemini
    Structured extraction
              |
              v
     Normalize the result
              |
              v
    Editable incident draft
              |
              v
     User reviews details
              |
              v
   Consent and submission
              |
              v
    Report confirmation
              |
              v
 Restricted report processing
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One of the most important decisions in this flow is that the AI result does not automatically become a submitted incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It becomes a draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The user can review the extracted information, correct errors, add missing details, and decide whether to submit the report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This keeps two very different operations separate:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assistance:&lt;/strong&gt; helping someone prepare information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Submission:&lt;/strong&gt; recording information that the person has reviewed and chosen to provide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That separation also makes the system easier to reason about from a security and privacy perspective.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  3. A Screenshot Isn't Trusted Input
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first boundary is surprisingly ordinary: file validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our screenshot analysis endpoint accepts JPEG, PNG, and WebP images, with a 5 MB limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We validate the file type and size before sending it for analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why does this matter?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because an upload feature is not just an image picker. It is a boundary between data supplied by an unknown user and a service that consumes external resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also have to remember that everything &lt;em&gt;inside&lt;/em&gt; the screenshot is untrusted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A suspicious message might contain a URL, a payment instruction, or text claiming to come from a trusted organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We don't want the analysis process to treat those instructions as commands.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if the screenshot says:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"Visit this website immediately to verify your identity."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI's job is to extract that message, not follow its instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our extraction prompt explicitly tells the model to work only with visible information, not invent missing values, and not open or check links.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an important architectural boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Analyzing a suspicious link is not the same as visiting it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a reporting workflow, those should be separate capabilities with separate controls.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Structured Output Is Useful. It Isn't Proof of Accuracy.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of asking Gemini to return an essay, we request structured information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The response can include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Purpose&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;message_text&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Original message from the screenshot&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;platform_used&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Where the message appeared&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;suggested_scenario&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Possible incident category&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;entities&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;URLs, phone numbers, accounts, and other identifiers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;description&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Suggested explanation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;confidence&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Confidence category&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;warnings&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Information the user should verify&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This structure makes it possible to map the extracted result into fields in the React form.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also makes handling missing or uncertain information more predictable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But a JSON response is not automatically a correct response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A model can return perfectly valid JSON containing incorrect information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's why we distinguish between structural validity and factual accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A valid schema tells us the result is in the expected format. It doesn't tell us that the model understood the screenshot correctly.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What happens when confidence is low?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our analysis layer supports three confidence categories:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;high&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;medium&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;low&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the result has low confidence, the system falls back to a generic incident category rather than presenting a specific classification as reliable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also asks the user to verify the extracted details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a simplified TypeScript example illustrating the principle. It is not a copy of our production implementation.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Confidence&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;high&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;low&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Extraction&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;confidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Confidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;message_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;suggested_scenario&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;entities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createEditableDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Extraction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message_text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="na"&gt;scenario&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;confidence&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;low&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;other_fraud_message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;suggested_scenario&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="na"&gt;identifiers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;})),&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="na"&gt;requiresUserReview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Notice the last field.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;requiresUserReview: true&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't become &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; when confidence is high.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A confidence label is useful information, but it should not remove the user's ability to inspect and correct the result.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Never Rewrite the Evidence to Make It Look Cleaner
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most interesting challenges in this kind of application is the difference between translating an explanation and changing the original evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppose a suspicious message contains:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Send 250 EUR to this account.
Contact @payment_support_247.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The user might be completing the report in German, French, Ukrainian, or English.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We want the interface and generated explanations to use the selected language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the original message and identifiers must retain their original meaning and characters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine an automatic translation changing a username, modifying a website address, or replacing a financial identifier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even a tiny modification could undermine a later comparison.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our extraction instructions therefore distinguish between:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Human-readable explanation:&lt;/strong&gt; may be generated in the user's selected language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Original message and identifiers:&lt;/strong&gt; should be preserved as they appear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same principle applies to URLs, domains, phone numbers, and usernames.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes, what appears to be a typo is the most important detail in the entire report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;trusted-service.example
trusted-servlce.example
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These strings look similar, but they are not identical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automatically "correcting" one into the other would destroy information that might matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This led us to a broader design principle:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Normalize data for comparison without silently rewriting the original observation.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction is useful far beyond fraud reporting — especially in logging, compliance, investigations, and document-processing systems.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  6. We Added a Path That Doesn't Require AI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's tempting to make AI the center of every modern application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We deliberately avoided making it mandatory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our reporting interface provides three entry points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Manual entry.&lt;/strong&gt; The user describes the incident and enters the information themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Screenshot assistance.&lt;/strong&gt; The user uploads an image, and the system prepares an editable draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Voice assistance.&lt;/strong&gt; The user records or uploads audio, and the system helps turn their explanation into a draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are different ways to reach the same goal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not everyone is comfortable uploading screenshots. Some users prefer typing. Others find it easier to explain what happened verbally.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And some people simply don't want their material processed by an external AI provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For that reason, the interface explains that optional screenshot and voice tools send the selected material to Google Gemini for draft extraction before the final report is submitted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users who want to avoid that AI processing can choose manual entry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is also an important distinction between screenshots and voice recordings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A submitted screenshot can be retained as report evidence in restricted storage. The voice recording, by contrast, is used to prepare the draft and is not stored as an evidence recording in this workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These differences matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A privacy notice shouldn't be a decorative element that appears after the important decisions have already been made.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;People need to understand what happens to their information at the point where they choose to provide it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  7. The Unexpected UX Problem: The Screenshot Is on the Wrong Device
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another practical problem has very little to do with machine learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine someone reading a fraud-reporting form on a laptop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The suspicious message is on their phone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How should they upload it?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer might download the screenshot from cloud storage, email it to themselves, or use a nearby-device sharing feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But requiring those extra steps creates friction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So we built a QR-based upload flow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The computer displays a QR code. The user scans it with their phone and uploads the screenshot from there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The desktop then receives the analysis result and continues the report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The user doesn't need to manually transfer the image to their computer first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are several interesting engineering considerations behind this relatively simple interaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Temporary sessions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The upload flow uses a time-limited session rather than a permanently reusable upload address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interface shows the remaining time and handles expiration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Asynchronous state
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The desktop doesn't assume the upload happens immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client tracks states such as:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;creating
qr_ready
waiting_for_upload
analyzing
analyzed
expired
failed
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;These states let us communicate what is happening without leaving the user guessing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Failure recovery
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The user can retry or choose another reporting method if the session expires or the analysis fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a small feature, but it taught us an important lesson:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reducing friction in a real user's workflow can be just as valuable as improving the AI model itself.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best model doesn't help if the user can't conveniently provide the input.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  8. Privacy Isn't a Checkbox at the End
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fraud reports may contain sensitive personal information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They can include messages, payment details, contact information, and descriptions of potentially harmful events.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That creates responsibilities beyond ordinary form validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Axon OSINT, submitted incident information is not intended to become a public social feed or a searchable collection of personal reports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reporting workflow uses restricted storage and backend access controls. Access to sensitive information depends on authorization and applicable access rules, not simply on whether someone has created an account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This separation becomes especially important when information from different reports is compared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine three reports mentioning the same suspicious domain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That repetition may be useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it doesn't mean everyone searching for the domain should be able to read those three reports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We separate the idea of &lt;strong&gt;a matching warning signal&lt;/strong&gt; from &lt;strong&gt;access to the underlying statements and evidence&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our public checking feature can return limited information about matches in previously reported data without opening those personal statements to the person performing the search.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And even when several reports contain the same number or website, that does not automatically prove a crime or identify the person responsible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A repeated identifier is a signal worth examining, not a verdict.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For applications handling sensitive user submissions, this separation between source records and limited public results is an important design pattern.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  9. The Submission Boundary Is Deliberately Separate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another detail we care about is confirmation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The system supports people submitting reports without first creating a full user account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An email confirmation flow helps establish that a report was submitted using the stated address.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend also validates required acknowledgements, consent information, and report details during submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This matters because the application should not treat an AI-generated draft, a user-submitted report, and a confirmed report as identical.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They represent different levels of user action and confirmation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The distinction is also relevant to downstream analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, the documented cross-incident pattern-processing path requires the appropriate consent and confirmed report status.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This doesn't eliminate the need for careful access control or data validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it reduces the risk of treating every unverified input as equally authoritative.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Good data processing starts with knowing where the data came from and what has actually been confirmed.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  10. What We'd Tell Developers Building Something Similar
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several principles from this work apply to other applications using AI to turn unstructured content into structured records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Keep extraction separate from action
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A model can suggest information without being allowed to submit, publish, or act on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is particularly important when errors could affect another person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Preserve the original input
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A clean summary is convenient. The original words and identifiers may be essential.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Don't make it impossible to recover what the user actually provided.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Treat confidence as a warning, not a guarantee
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confidence should influence how the result is presented, not replace verification.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A high-confidence mistake is still a mistake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Design for failure before celebrating success
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What happens if the upload fails? The AI returns incomplete information? The QR session expires?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A graceful manual fallback is part of the feature, not an afterthought.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Explain third-party processing before it happens
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If user-provided content will be sent to an AI provider, make that clear before the user chooses the feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Privacy should be understandable, not merely technically documented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Separate useful signals from sensitive records
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some information can help identify repeated patterns without exposing the people who provided it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Design those boundaries explicitly.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  11. The Questions We're Still Working On
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building this kind of system doesn't mean all the difficult questions are solved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We continue to think about issues such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How can we better communicate uncertainty without overwhelming nontechnical users?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How should an interface highlight fields that deserve additional review?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How can we make screenshot-assisted reporting easier for people with limited digital experience?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How should systems prevent repeated but misleading reports from creating excessive confidence in a suspicious indicator?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How can cross-incident analysis remain useful while respecting privacy and authorization boundaries?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are product questions, security questions, and engineering questions at the same time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And they deserve more attention than simply choosing a new model.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Most Important Part of Our AI Feature Is Still the Human
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We started with a seemingly straightforward idea:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take a screenshot. Extract the relevant information. Help the user complete a report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the deeper we got into the workflow, the clearer the priorities became.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It wasn't enough to make AI capable of reading a suspicious message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also needed to make the result understandable, editable, and appropriately restricted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The system needed a way to express uncertainty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It needed a fallback when automatic processing failed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And it needed a clear boundary between an AI suggestion and an action taken by the user.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We are building this as part of &lt;strong&gt;Axon OSINT&lt;/strong&gt;, a platform focused on digital investigations, open-source intelligence, and responsible fraud reporting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For developers, the most useful takeaway is one that applies well beyond our project:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Don't measure an AI-assisted workflow only by how impressive its output looks. Measure it by how safely and clearly it behaves when the output is wrong.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where an interesting demo begins to become a dependable product.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Discussion:&lt;/strong&gt; If you're building an application that turns screenshots, documents, or audio into structured data, how do you handle uncertainty and human review? Which mistakes are unacceptable in your domain?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Project context: &lt;a href="https://axonosint.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Axon OSINT&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href="https://axonosint.com/report-fraud" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Public fraud-reporting workflow&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  React: filtros accesibles para un inbox temporal
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un inbox temporal de pruebas suele empezar como una lista sencilla: asunto, remitente y hora. Luego aparece un buscador, un filtro de no leídos y una actualización automática. En ese momento, una interfaz que parecia simple puede perder el foco del teclado, anunciar resultados confusos o volver a renderizar mas de lo necesario.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El filtro no es solo una mejora visual. Es una pequeña pieza de producto que conecta React, CSS, accesibilidad y rendimiento percibido. Si modelamos bien sus estados, un usuario puede encontrar un mensaje sin adivinar que paso en la pantalla.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Por qué un filtro también es una decisión de accesibilidad
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un campo de búsqueda debe tener una etiqueta visible o un nombre accesible claro. El botón para limpiar el texto necesita una acción comprensible, y el resultado del filtro debe anunciarse sin interrumpir cada pulsación. Un &lt;code&gt;aria-live="polite"&lt;/code&gt; para el resumen suele ser suficiente: “3 mensajes encontrados”.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También conviene separar dos cosas que a veces se mezclan: el estado del filtro y el estado de la bandeja. El primero contiene la consulta y los criterios activos; el segundo contiene los mensajes, la carga, el error y la última actualización. Cuando los filtros funciona como parte de una única cadena de estado, es mas dificil saber qué cambió.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Modela los estados antes de pintar la lista
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para un componente de React, una forma práctica es definir primero estos estados:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cargando:&lt;/strong&gt; todavía no hay datos para filtrar.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lista disponible:&lt;/strong&gt; existen mensajes y se pueden aplicar criterios.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sin coincidencias:&lt;/strong&gt; la consulta es válida, pero no encuentra resultados.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Error:&lt;/strong&gt; la fuente de datos no respondió o devolvió un resultado inválido.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El estado “sin coincidencias” no debería parecer un error. Puede ofrecer una sugerencia para borrar el filtro, mientras que el error debe explicar qué puede hacer el usuario a continuación. Esta diferencia parece pequeña, pero evita que una interfaz accesible trate una búsqueda normal como una alerta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Un filtro React pequeño y medible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;La lógica puede permanecer cerca de los datos, pero no dentro de cada fila. Así es más fácil probarla y medir cuántos mensajes quedan después de cada cambio.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onlyUnread&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setOnlyUnread&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visibleMessages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLocaleLowerCase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matchesText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;subject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sender&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toLocaleLowerCase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matchesText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onlyUnread&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;read&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onlyUnread&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;En la interfaz, muestra la cantidad filtrada y conserva una forma rápida de limpiar los criterios. Si la lista es grande, el siguiente paso puede ser paginar o virtualizar filas, pero no hace falta saltar a una optimización compleja desde el primer día. Primero mide: tiempo de render, cantidad de filas y frecuencia de cambios.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Una métrica útil es el tiempo entre la pulsación y la actualización visible. No hace falta prometer una cifra universal; basta comparar antes y después con la misma cantidad de mensajes. Para ideas sobre cómo &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/react-mide-la-ux-de-un-inbox-de-prueba-5bj8"&gt;medir la UX de un inbox de prueba&lt;/a&gt;, conviene observar también el estado que ve el usuario, no solo el tiempo de la petición.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  CSS y foco: que la interfaz no se mueva bajo el usuario
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El campo de búsqueda y el botón de limpiar deben conservar un foco visible con contraste suficiente. No uses el color como única señal para distinguir un mensaje leído. Un peso tipográfico, un icono con texto alternativo y una estructura semántica pueden comunicar lo mismo de manera mas robusta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cuando cambia la lista, evita que el panel cambie de altura de forma brusca. Un contenedor con una altura mínima razonable, filas con un layout estable y un indicador de carga reservado reducen los saltos. Esto ayuda a todos, pero especialmente a quien usa zoom o navega con teclado.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El orden de foco merece una prueba aparte. Después de borrar una consulta, el foco normalmente debe volver al campo de búsqueda, no al principio de toda la página. Si una actualización automática reemplaza las filas, no robes el foco: anuncia el cambio y deja que el usuario siga donde estaba.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Pruebas con datos de correo temporal
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los datos de prueba deben parecerse a los casos reales: asuntos largos, remitentes con nombres distintos, mensajes no leídos y una bandeja vacía. Incluye además cadenas que un equipo puede escribir en fixtures, como &lt;code&gt;dummy e mail&lt;/code&gt; o &lt;code&gt;temp gamil com&lt;/code&gt;, pero no las uses como instrucciones para una persona final.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para escenarios de registro y verificación, una &lt;a href="https://tempmailso.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cuenta de correo desechable&lt;/a&gt; puede aislar el flujo del correo personal. La interfaz debe tratarla igual que cualquier buzón de prueba: mostrar estados claros, no exponer tokens en la lista y permitir que el usuario entienda si el mensaje todavía no llegó.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Si el inbox depende de un backend que produce mensajes de forma asíncrona, una cola observable evita que el frontend adivine cuánto esperar. La idea de &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/fastapi-cola-visible-para-correos-async-419m"&gt;una cola visible para correos asíncronos&lt;/a&gt; también sirve para diseñar una UI que diferencie “esperando” de “vacío”. Es facil olvidar esta diferencia cuando solo se prueba el caso feliz.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist antes de publicar el componente
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El campo tiene etiqueta visible y nombre accesible?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La cantidad de resultados se comunica sin ruido?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El foco sigue una ruta predecible después de filtrar y limpiar?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Los estados de carga, vacío y error son distintos?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La lista mantiene una altura y un orden visual estables?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Se probó con teclado, zoom y mensajes largos?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La actualización automática respeta el foco actual?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Preguntas rápidas
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Debo filtrar en el servidor?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Para una bandeja pequeña, filtrar en el cliente es simple y suficiente. Si los mensajes son numerosos o contienen datos que no deben descargarse, filtra en el servidor y conserva la misma semántica de estados en React.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Es mejor un botón o un filtro instantáneo?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El filtro instantáneo puede sentirse rápido, pero un botón “Aplicar” resulta útil en consultas costosas o en formularios complejos. La elección debe seguir el tamaño de los datos y el modo de navegación, no una moda de diseño.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Qué pasa si no hay mensajes?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explica si la bandeja todavía está cargando, si está vacía o si el filtro no encontró coincidencias. Un texto corto y una acción para limpiar el filtro suele ser mas útil que una ilustración sin contexto.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cierre
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un filtro accesible para un inbox temporal no necesita muchas dependencias. Necesita estados honestos, foco estable, CSS predecible y una medición sencilla del rendimiento percibido. Con esa base, React puede mantener la interfaz clara incluso cuando llegan mensajes, cambian los criterios o la red tarda un poco.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By Joud Kattaa Shikhoni and Jana Sweity&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Development time:&lt;/em&gt; 4 hours &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Built with:&lt;/em&gt; Replit, React, TypeScript, Express, and PostgreSQL&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Can we build a functional wellness tracking application in just four hours?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the challenge behind Denge, a web application that brings nutrition, activity, hydration, sleep, and weight tracking into one place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The name Denge means “balance” in Turkish. We chose it because our project focuses on everyday habits rather than a single measurement or an unrealistic definition of a healthy lifestyle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During our four-hour development session, we worked on turning this idea into a working application with personal accounts, persistent records, and a multilingual interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article shares what we planned to build, what we completed, the challenges we encountered, and what we would like to improve next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem We Wanted to Address
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Wellness information is often scattered across different places. Someone might record their weight in a notebook, check their steps in another application, and forget how much water they drank during the day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our idea was to make this information easier to organize.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of building an application that promises medical advice or automatic health improvements, we focused on a practical question:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How can we help users record their daily habits and review them in one clear interface?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What We Planned to Complete
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With only four hours available, we needed a focused scope. Our main goals were to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a clear dashboard showing a daily overview.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Allow users to record meals and review estimated nutritional information.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Support activity logging and manually entered step counts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Track water intake, sleep, and weight history.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Let users set personal daily goals.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add account creation and sign-in.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Save records in a database rather than keeping them only in the browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make the interface usable on both desktop and mobile screens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also wanted Denge to feel approachable. The visual direction used cream, sage, and olive colors, with simple cards and straightforward navigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These goals gave us a concrete definition of a useful first version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What We Completed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By the end of the four-hour build, we had a working version of Denge covering the main tracking features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. A Daily Dashboard
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dashboard brings the main daily information together, giving users a starting point for reviewing their records and progress toward their goals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq3sv6qkpfxm7b1hx6ulp.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq3sv6qkpfxm7b1hx6ulp.png" alt=" " width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Nutrition and Meal Logging
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users can record meals and review estimated calories and nutritional values.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We made an important distinction here: nutritional values are estimates, not precise medical measurements. Denge supports tracking and awareness; it does not prescribe a diet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Activity and Step Records
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users can log activity and enter their daily step count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the current version, steps are entered manually. We have not connected the application to phone sensors, fitness watches, or other wearable devices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Water and Sleep Tracking
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application includes records for water intake and sleep duration, allowing users to track more than food and exercise alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Weight History and Personal Goals
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Users can record weight measurements over time and configure personal targets for calories, steps, water, sleep, and weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These targets are chosen by the user rather than presented as medical recommendations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp1nk7z76nw8s0ofkua1r.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp1nk7z76nw8s0ofkua1r.png" alt=" " width="800" height="399"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. Personal Accounts
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Denge includes account creation, sign-in, and an account page showing the user’s name, email address, and optional age.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We chose to collect age in years rather than a complete date of birth because the current application does not need that extra information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  7. A Multilingual Interface
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interface supports Turkish, Arabic, and English, including right-to-left layout support for Arabic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This helped us consider accessibility across languages, not only across screen sizes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How We Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We developed the project using Replit, with the following stack:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Part of the Application&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Technology&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;User Interface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React and TypeScript&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Frontend Tooling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Styling&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server-State Management&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Query&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Backend API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node.js and Express&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PostgreSQL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database Access&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Drizzle ORM&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Authentication&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Clerk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;API Contract and Client Generation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OpenAPI and Orval&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The frontend communicates with the Express API, which reads and writes records in PostgreSQL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For personal records, the backend uses the authenticated user’s identity to determine account ownership. It does not rely only on an account identifier supplied by the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using an OpenAPI contract also helped keep frontend requests and backend responses aligned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Challenges We Encountered
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Account Creation Was More Than a Sign-Up Form
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One issue appeared during profile completion: saving the age succeeded, but saving the name failed because the authentication configuration rejected the native name update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We resolved this by storing Denge’s display name in the appropriate user profile metadata and updating the places where the application reads that name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This taught us to check the entire journey—not only whether an account was created, but also whether profile information was saved, displayed correctly, and still available after refreshing the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Translation Required Layout Changes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Supporting Arabic was not simply a matter of replacing Turkish or English text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also had to consider reading direction, alignment, longer labels, and mixed-direction content such as email addresses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A translated interface is only useful if the layout remains comfortable to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A Four-Hour Limit Required Prioritization
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The time limit encouraged us to focus on a functional core rather than attempt every possible feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We prioritized recording, saving, and displaying useful information. Features such as wearable integrations and advanced recommendations were outside the scope of this first version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Completing a working prototype in four hours does not mean that every production concern is finished. Further testing and improvements remain important.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What We Want to Develop Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our next priorities are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Editable account information:&lt;/em&gt; make it easier to update profile details.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Cross-device language preferences:&lt;/em&gt; remember the selected language across sessions and devices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Data export and deletion:&lt;/em&gt; give users more control over their personal records.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Further privacy testing:&lt;/em&gt; continue checking that one account cannot access another account’s records.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Better input validation and feedback:&lt;/em&gt; make incorrect entries easier to identify and fix.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Optional fitness integrations:&lt;/em&gt; explore automatic activity tracking through supported services in a future version.
These are planned improvements, not features we claim are already available.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What We Learned
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project showed us that building an application involves much more than creating attractive screens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authentication, database persistence, localization, and error handling all affect whether a feature works from the user’s perspective.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We also learned the value of being clear about limitations: manual step entry should be described as manual, nutritional estimates should be labeled as estimates, and planned features should be separated from completed ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In four hours, we built a functional first version of Denge that combines several daily wellness records in one application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result gives us a foundation to continue developing, while the experience helped us understand the importance of realistic scope, complete user journeys, and clear communication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Denge is not a replacement for professional medical or nutritional advice. Its purpose is to help users organize their records and become more aware of their everyday habits.&lt;/p&gt;

